# <type>(<scope>): <subject>   ← 50 chars max, imperative mood, no period
#
# <body>                        ← wrap at 72 chars; explain *why*, not *what*
#
# <footer>                      ← BREAKING CHANGE / issue refs / co-authors
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Conventional Commits — https://www.conventionalcommits.org
#
# Type (required):
#   feat      — new user-facing feature
#   fix       — bug fix
#   refactor  — code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature
#   perf      — performance improvement
#   test      — adding or correcting tests
#   docs      — documentation only
#   style     — formatting, whitespace, semicolons (no code logic)
#   chore     — maintenance, deps, tooling (no src or test change)
#   ci        — CI/CD pipeline, GitHub Actions, workflows
#   build     — build system, packaging
#   revert    — revert a previous commit
#
# Scope (optional, lowercase, project-specific). Examples seen in this repo:
#   accounting, stock, hooks, api, patches, json, tests, ci, changelog,
#   merge, deps, customer, employee, item, sales-order, warehouse, uom
#
# Subject rules:
#   • imperative mood: "add", "fix", "rename" — not "added"/"adds"
#   • no trailing period
#   • lowercase first word (except proper nouns / DocType names)
#
# Body (optional, but encouraged for non-trivial changes):
#   • explain motivation and contrast with prior behavior
#   • reference affected DocTypes, hooks, or modules
#   • note any side effects or migration steps
#
# Footer (optional):
#   BREAKING CHANGE: <description of the break and migration path>
#   Refs: #123
#   Closes: #456
#   Co-Authored-By: Name <email@example.com>
#
# Examples:
#   feat(accounting): add Accounting Department module
#   fix(tests): wire before_tests hook and align naming_series defaults
#   refactor: rename cd_stock to connect_diagnostics_stock
#   chore(deps): bump frappe to v15.42
#
# One-time setup (per clone):
#   git config --local commit.template .gitmessage
